eenhouse gases and if the  power comes from wind  there's only three kilograms of harmful  emissions per  lab grown diamond carrot well with me  now is dale vince the founder of sky  diamond a company which says it's  tackling the climate crisis by creating  beautiful ethical diamonds  using green technology with no carbon  footprint so welcome to you  as we heard there dale vince creating  diamonds in a lab uses a huge amount of  power so how do you make sure that the  process is carbon neutral  well our process uh just has uh  four simple ingredients we use the rain  and the sun  sorry if we use the wind and the sun to  make energy to power our process we  capture rain and we split that to make  hydrogen  and we take carbon directly from the  atmosphere which is the core ingredient  the only ingredient really the diamond  in fact so the winter sun the rain and  carbon from the atmosphere that's all we  use so our process is not carbon neutral  or  or carbon zero it's carbon negative we  embed  carbon from the atmosphere into our  stones we've commissioned a report by  imperial college it's an independent one  the first  in the world and that shows 1100 tons of  rock have to be dug to make a single  carat of stone  and our own energy footprint is 40 units  of electricity not 250 but 40. that's  like  three days typical household use in  britain  i guess how green a lab-grown diamond is  depends on the kind of powder you use if  it's fossil fuels it's going to cause  problems  to mine 1100 tons of stone you have to  use a lot of fossil fuels i think this  is a myth  from the mining industry it's a  defensive measure they put out these  reports a few years ago i think the  numbers are very  unreliable um you know we're using  machines that are available in the world  today to make our stones that's not  special it is only 40 units of  electricity  and um you know by comparison the uh  greenhouse gas emissions for  for a carrot of mine stone is half a ton  and the carbon footprint is a hundred  kilograms you know  these are um these are this is data from  imperial college who've  you know put a lot of time into this to  get an independent view of the mind  industry  and um you know it's about time we kind  of shine a light on this because there's  a lot said about  lab grown stones having a high energy  content and i think it's not true  and certainly in our experience it's  absolutely not true  and what about the the end product can  you tell the difference between a lab  grown diamond  and one that's traditionally mined  if you're in a lab and you stick it  under a microscope you can see a  difference and the difference  is that their lab growth stone is more  perfect  but if you're a jeweler or a member of  the public somebody looking at a piece  of jewelry absolutely you can't tell  they're chemically identical  and almost molecularly identical but the  lab grown is slightly more perfect  dale vince very interesting to talk to  you thanks very much indeed  now carbon dioxide emissions from fossil  fuel combustion in the eu  dropped 10 last year eurostat the eu  statistical office found that emissions  fell in  all of the eu's 27 member states  compared with 2019 as governments  imposed lockdowns  due to covert 19. and here on our data  dashboard you can see  how co2 emissions around the world are  ticking up and that of course has an  impact on this  number here this is human-induced global  warming that's how much our planet  has warmed since the industrial  revolution  and then up here you can see the  real-time  power mix in great britain and around 47  of our electricity is currently coming  from fossil fuels 34  from renewable sources and 19 there from  nuclear and you can see a more detailed  breakdown of the real-time power makes  including just how much electricity is  coming from sources including wind and  solar  anytime you like just head to our  website skynews.com  climate now let's take a look at some of  those other climate news now  and every household in england will  receive separate  weekly food collections from 2023  to help people recycle their waste it's  part of plans by the government to reach  their target of eliminating  all avoidable waste by 2050. ministers  are also considering  free garden waste collections which  could save homeowners  over a hundred million pounds a year in  charges  [Applause]  the national trust is reconstructing a  19th century parkland in norfolk using  aerial photos taken by the raf after the  second world  war work is already underway at oxbra  hall to restore 175 of the original  400 acres of woodland making it one of  the largest habitats the charity has  ever created  the conservation team hope it'll make  the area more resilient to climate  change  and biologists in france are playing  their very own game of where's wally  forget the stripey t-shirt and bobble  hat wally the gray whale has been  spotted in the mediterranean  thousands of miles from his natural  habitat in the pacific ocean  experts think he accidentally swam into  the atlantic ocean  after global warming melted ice and  opened up a route through  now the price of plastic bags in england  is being doubled to  10 pence all businesses including corner  shops will have to apply the charge  from the 21st of may previously only  retailers with more than 250  employees were required to do so so the  five pence charge was introduced in  england  in 2015. since then their use has fallen  by  95 the average shopper  now only buys four single-use plastic  bags a year  and that's compared with 140 in 2014.  the government says it hopes a new cost  will reduce the use of plastic bags by  up to 80 percent  in small and medium-sized shops  well campaigner lizzie carr founded the  group planet patrol  and thinks the tempe charge doesn't go  far enough  she's calling for a total ban on plastic  bags including  bags for life one major retailer  actually reported a two thousand  seven hundred and forty percent increase  in back for life sales over that four  year  levy period so these figures show that  people really aren't deterred by a  charge we need to see  more transparent reporting that's across  all retailers  large and small and we need to see this  fed back to the general public more  accurately and not  completely emitting bag for life sales  which we know have skyrocketed  now they're one of britain's most  recognizable and well-loved  mammals but hedgehogs are vulnerable to  extinction in the uk  gardens with decking and artificial turf  a loss of natural habitat  and even climate change are all posing a  threat to the spiny creatures  well in an effort to help a campaign is  aiming to create britain's first  hedgehog friendly borough school  children in surrey heath are being given  everything they need to create a safe  haven for the endangered  mammal well queen guitarist brian may is  part of the campaign and he joins me now  brian may thanks so much for talking to  us  tell us a little bit more about this  campaign you've got going in schools  in that area what are you teaching the  children and what do you want to achieve  well children are wonderful actually  because they do get it immediately  and we're very much in the schools in  surrey heath and they're helping us do  a survey a census of hedgehogs in the  area and it gets them into this feeling  that  these animals are precious and that  they're threatened and they are the next  generation who will have to look after  them  i mean we do make a big noise about uh  how people treat their  their their wildlife population in other  countries but we're really not doing a  very good job in this country  and hedgehogs are massively in decline  from about 30 million to under 1 million  now  it's a very serious thing and it's not  just they're cute creatures i mean these  are the  the wildlife of britain and if you make  your  your country your countryside and your  gardens etc  friendly to hedgehogs then they'll be  friendly to all the wild animals that we  have in britain so it's a very very  serious cause  as the habitat gets eaten up more and  more by housing and urbanization  concrete  i mean it's clear that you're passionate  about conservation you're passionate  about the environment and have been for  years i just want to get your view  on musicians touring because i know  coldplay for example decided not to tour  their album just over a year ago  because of the the carbon footprint  involved is that something that queen  would ever consider doing  well we have considered it yes we've  postponed our touring twice now so we're  now due to go out next may and june  and we're very much looking at lessening  our carbon footprint yeah i think people  will still have to travel if you would  if you if you play music you have to get  to people somehow it's not good enough  just to be on zoom or  or tv or the internet or whatever you  have to make personal contact but  yes you can be a lot more careful about  how much energy you use how much  pollution you create  what your carbon um impression is  so yes we're looking at it and we'll do  the best we can we already did actually  on the last tour we did  um which was all around um southeast  asia  we cut down the amount of trucks that we  use to about half so  it's it it's there it's coming brian may  thanks very much indeed  that's it for today's show but you can  get your climate fix over the weekend  with our podcast  on this week's episode we're talking  tipping points and how it's not too late  to save the planet that's on climate  cast and it's available wherever you  normally get your podcast from  and on our weekly digital show this week  sky correspondent siobhan robbins tells  us the behind the scenes story  of thailand's fight against toxic  wildfires  that's available on sky news social  channels our app and our website  thanks for watching and see you next  week  [Music]  you

